Purpose & context Amruta is writing a novel called Forget Me Not — a character-driven ensemble fiction set primarily in Mumbai, spanning approximately a decade with a non-linear, multi-POV structure. The story follows a close-knit friend group whose relationships evolve through love, grief, guilt, loss, and chosen family dynamics. The central narrative arc involves Amy and Leo (married, with a major amnesia arc at the story's core), Nick and Miles (a relationship with a long, complicated history), and Abhi and Tanu (with their own emotional costs around leaving Mumbai for London). Secondary characters include Ash, Vic, and Akki. Key relationship dynamics: Leo is deeply in love with Amy and expresses it through quiet, consistent action; Amy's love for Leo is demonstrated through behavior rather than declaration and is a deliberate craft focus. Nick and Amy share an intense platonic friendship; Nick carries unrequited feelings for Miles over many years before she develops feelings for him through her own perception. Abhi functions as an older-brother figure to Amy and has his own complex history with her that has been resolved. The novel has a central amnesia arc: Amy is injured in a car accident and wakes having lost six years of memories, including her marriage to Leo. This is the structural engine of much of the current writing. Amruta writes primarily on her phone, currently drafts chapters on Blogger, and uses Notion (connected via MCP) as her chapter timeline and organisational hub. --- Current state Active writing work spans multiple scenes across the story's timeline: Amy's emotional arc toward Leo is an identified structural gap being actively addressed — scenes are being written to show Amy's love through action (late-night vigils, panic at unreachability) rather than exposition, building toward the amnesia arc's emotional payoff. Reconciliation scenes (Leo/Amy post-breakup) have been drafted and refined, with Amy's argument to Leo strengthened into a two-part logical dismantling rather than an emotional appeal. Nick's arc includes: scenes before his fake death (emotional stakes, Miles as sole confidante), his return/reunion with the group, and his relationship with Miles. A scene of Nick keeping vigil at Amy's hospital bedside has been written. The amnesia arc has scenes drafted: Amy's first night home (Abhi in the armchair, Leo outside the door, mango ice cream on the couch), the hospital and car-ride home sequences, and Leo's experience of being a stranger to his own wife. A Bangalore airport scene (pre-relationship, delayed flight) was written featuring Amy, Nick, and Leo texting from Mumbai — establishing early emotional dynamics and a parallel between Amy's and Nick's respective unrequited situations. The Notion Chapter Timeline database ("Forget Me Not — Chapter Timeline") is set up with fields for Book Order, POV, Timeline Period, Scene Type, and Status. Chapter 1 (Leo, Hospital, Present Day) is the first entry. --- On the horizon Suggested future scenes for Amy's arc that haven't yet been written include: a monsoon night scene, a domestic reciprocal sticky-note gesture, the night before the wedding, Amy's memory-return moment, and a scene where Amy quietly rearranges her life for Leo without telling him. Open structural question: the novel's tooling setup (local offline web app with React/FastAPI/Git for dual-timeline management) was architected in one conversation but not yet built. Claude recommended prioritising writing first and building tooling incrementally, starting with well-structured markdown or the existing Blogger/Notion workflow. --- Key learnings & principles Show, don't state is the governing principle: emotion is carried through physical gesture, specific domestic detail, and action. Characters express love through doing (keeping vigil, driving under false pretexts, bringing tea, staying awake) rather than declaring. Restraint over sentiment: when Claude over-explains or makes emotional beats too loud, Amruta flags it. Speeches that do too much explanatory work get cut or tightened. Comic relief as structural tool: humor and lightness are used to release tension after vulnerable moments, not to undercut them. Internal narration is observational, not declarative: POV characters notice and register; they do not announce what they feel. Silence and omission carry weight: what characters don't say is as important as what they do. Two separate medical arcs for Amy must not be conflated: an early detection (fully resolved, quiet fear) and a later recurrence (the scene of real collapse) are distinct in emotional register. Character continuity errors are flagged directly: Amruta corrects factual errors (e.g., Nick tells no one but Miles about the fake death — not Leo) concisely and expects immediate, accurate adjustment. --- Approach & patterns Amruta provides raw draft material — often note-form prompts or rough scenes — and asks Claude to elevate, complete, or expand while preserving her own phrasing and character choices. Feedback is brief and directional ("not dramatic enough," "rewrite," "make it more emotional") rather than detailed — Claude is expected to interpret and execute. Specific weaknesses are identified rather than requesting wholesale changes, suggesting a clear vision with targeted gaps. Claude is sometimes asked to review a scene critically before rewriting — this is a valued step, not a preamble to skip. POV consistency matters: scenes are assigned to specific narrators and Amruta will flag if the wrong character is centred. The character map (CHARACTERMAP_MASTER.txt) is the canonical continuity reference, maintained as a project file and updated when new canon is established. --- Tools & resources Blogger: primary drafting platform (mobile-friendly) Notion: chapter timeline and organisational hub, connected via MCP (database: "Forget Me Not — Chapter Timeline," ID ac0f5ffe43f9487299f3e62415aa4f74; data source ID 86c9e6f0-3457-4828-a9df-ee5610